//! A double-ended queue built from two growable lists.
//!
//! `Deque<T>` supports pushing and popping at BOTH ends in amortised O(1)
//! time. It is the standard "two stacks back to back" construction: one
//! [`List`] holds the front half and another holds the back half, each used as
//! a stack (push and pop at its tail, which is the cheap end of a [`List`]).
//! The logical order of the deque runs from the tail of `front`, down to the
//! head of `front`, then the head of `back`, up to the tail of `back`. In other
//! words `front` is stored reversed so that the deque's front element sits at
//! the cheap end of the list.
//!
//! Why two lists rather than one shared buffer with head/tail indices: a plain
//! [`List`] here is a tail-only stack (push and pop at the end), so a single
//! list cannot pop from its head cheaply. Splitting into two lists gives a cheap
//! end to each side of the deque. The catch is that when the side you want to
//! pop from is empty, all the elements are sitting in the OTHER list, so they
//! have to be moved across. That move is done by [`Deque.flip`], and because
//! each element is flipped at most once before it is popped, the cost averages
//! out to O(1) per operation even though a single flip is O(n).
//!
//! The invariant that makes this work: at any time an element lives in exactly
//! one of the two lists, never both, and `size()` is simply the sum of the two
//! sizes. A flip empties one list into the other, so it preserves that
//! invariant and the total element count.
//!
//! The deque is a `class`, so it has reference semantics: passing a `Deque`
//! around shares the same underlying lists rather than copying them. It fits
//! alongside the other `collections` containers ([`List`], and the map and set
//! types) as a general-purpose ordered container for queue and stack style
//! workloads.

import list;

/// A double-ended queue over elements of type `T`, backed by two [`List`]s.
///
/// See the file header for the storage model. Briefly: [`Deque.front`] holds
/// the front portion in reversed order and [`Deque.back`] holds the back
/// portion in natural order, each used as a tail-only stack. Every element is
/// in exactly one of the two lists. Construct with [`Deque.init`] or the free
/// helper [`newDeque`].
pub class Deque<T> {
    /// The front portion of the deque, stored REVERSED.
    ///
    /// The deque's frontmost element is the tail of this list, so pushing and
    /// popping the front is a cheap tail operation. When this list is empty but
    /// the deque is not, the elements are all in [`Deque.back`] and get moved
    /// here by [`Deque.flip`] on the next front access.
    pub front: List<T>,
    /// The back portion of the deque, stored in NATURAL order.
    ///
    /// The deque's backmost element is the tail of this list, so pushing and
    /// popping the back is a cheap tail operation. When this list is empty but
    /// the deque is not, the elements are all in [`Deque.front`] and get moved
    /// here by [`Deque.flip`] on the next back access.
    pub back: List<T>,

    /// Creates an empty deque with two fresh, empty backing lists.
    init() {
        self.front = List<T>();
        self.back = List<T>();
    }

    /// Total number of elements, counting both ends.
    ///
    /// Because every element lives in exactly one of the two lists, this is just
    /// the sum of their sizes and needs no separate counter.
    pub fn size(self: Deque<T>): int { return self.front.size() + self.back.size(); }
    /// Whether the deque holds no elements at all, i.e. [`Deque.size`] is zero.
    pub fn isEmpty(self: Deque<T>): bool { return self.size() == 0; }

    /// Adds `v` at the front of the deque in O(1).
    ///
    /// This is a plain tail push on [`Deque.front`]; no flip is ever needed to
    /// insert, only to pop or peek from an empty side.
    pub fn pushFront(self: Deque<T>, v: T): void { self.front.push(v); }
    /// Adds `v` at the back of the deque in O(1).
    ///
    /// This is a plain tail push on [`Deque.back`]; see [`Deque.pushFront`].
    pub fn pushBack(self: Deque<T>, v: T): void { self.back.push(v); }

    /// Moves every element of `src` into `dst`, reversing their order.
    ///
    /// This is the rebalancing step that lets both ends stay cheap. It repeatedly
    /// pops the tail of `src` and pushes it onto the tail of `dst`, which reverses
    /// the sequence: exactly what is needed to turn one side's storage order into
    /// the other's. It is called only when the side being read is empty, so a
    /// flip touches each element at most once between insertion and removal,
    /// which is what keeps the pop and peek operations amortised O(1) despite a
    /// single flip being O(n).
    ///
    /// The `v ?? src.get(0)` guards an unwrap that has already been proven safe:
    /// the surrounding `if (v != undefined)` establishes that `v` is present, so
    /// the `?? src.get(0)` fallback is never actually taken and exists only to
    /// satisfy the optional-to-`T` conversion for [`List.push`].
    fn flip(self: Deque<T>, src: List<T>, dst: List<T>): void {
        while (src.size() > 0) {
            let v = src.pop();
            if (v != undefined) { dst.push(v ?? src.get(0)); }
        }
    }

    /// Removes and returns the frontmost element, or `undefined` if empty.
    ///
    /// If [`Deque.front`] has run empty, the elements are all in [`Deque.back`],
    /// so it flips them across first (see [`Deque.flip`]) and then pops. When the
    /// whole deque is empty both lists are empty, the flip does nothing, and the
    /// final `pop` returns `undefined`.
    pub fn popFront(self: Deque<T>): T | undefined {
        if (self.front.size() == 0) {
            self.flip(self.back, self.front);
        }
        return self.front.pop();
    }

    /// Removes and returns the backmost element, or `undefined` if empty.
    ///
    /// The mirror of [`Deque.popFront`]: it flips [`Deque.front`] into
    /// [`Deque.back`] when the back has run empty, then pops the back.
    pub fn popBack(self: Deque<T>): T | undefined {
        if (self.back.size() == 0) {
            self.flip(self.front, self.back);
        }
        return self.back.pop();
    }

    /// Returns the frontmost element without removing it, or `undefined` if empty.
    ///
    /// Like [`Deque.popFront`] it may trigger a flip to bring the front element
    /// into [`Deque.front`], but it reads with [`List.last`] instead of popping,
    /// so the element stays in place. Note that the flip is not free: a peek on an
    /// empty front side still moves the whole back list across.
    pub fn peekFront(self: Deque<T>): T | undefined {
        if (self.front.size() == 0) { self.flip(self.back, self.front); }
        return self.front.last();
    }

    /// Returns the backmost element without removing it, or `undefined` if empty.
    ///
    /// The mirror of [`Deque.peekFront`]: it may flip [`Deque.front`] into
    /// [`Deque.back`] and then reads the tail without removing it.
    pub fn peekBack(self: Deque<T>): T | undefined {
        if (self.back.size() == 0) { self.flip(self.front, self.back); }
        return self.back.last();
    }
}

/// Constructs an empty [`Deque`] of element type `T`.
///
/// A convenience wrapper over [`Deque.init`] for call sites that prefer a free
/// function to a constructor call.
pub fn newDeque<T>(): Deque<T> { return Deque<T>(); }
